# The Grain, Episode One, Movement I — The office.
#
# Scene 1 of the novel, played straight. No choices: the movement exists to put
# the read on screen before the game asks anything of the player. Every line is
# lifted from `story/snapshot-2026-09-03/script/chapter-01-one-person-there.fountain`.
#
# Henry is never drawn. Ruth holds `center` for the whole scene, because there is
# nobody else in the room and the office is two people and two cups.


label office_closed:
    stage calder_office_late_afternoon
    play office

    "At five thirty-seven, Henry Calder turns the card in the frosted-glass door from OPEN to CLOSED. It has said CLOSED for most of the afternoon. Turning it is less an announcement than a ritual."

    "Ruth Ellery knocks directly beneath his painted name."

    henry "It's open."

    show ruth composed at center

    ruth "It says closed."

    henry "Then why did you knock?"

    ruth dry "I remembered you were a liar."

    "Henry opens the door. Ruth looks first at him, then at the room: two client chairs, one filing cabinet, a telephone, a fan moving warm air from one side of the office to the other."

    ruth "Your name has gotten smaller."

    henry "The door was replaced."

    ruth "I remember the door."

    henry "Then my name got smaller."

    "She takes the chair that does not face the window. Henry notices this and sits behind neither chair nor desk. He rests against the filing cabinet."

    jump the_coffee


label the_coffee:
    henry "Coffee?"

    ruth dry "At this hour?"

    henry "It's the same coffee as this morning."

    ruth "That wasn't my concern."

    henry "Good."

    "He pours one cup. Ruth accepts it despite herself, smells it, and sets it on the floor."

    henry "You came all this way to insult the office."

    ruth composed "No. I need you for dinner."

    henry "That's not how people usually phrase it."

    ruth "I'm out of practice."

    henry "With dinner?"

    ruth exposed "With needing you."

    "Henry waits. Ruth had prepared for a question. His silence makes her discard the answer."

    jump the_invitation


label the_invitation:
    ruth composed "Tolland's. The Winter Room. Eight o'clock."

    henry "The department store?"

    ruth "After closing."

    henry "Is it a robbery?"

    ruth "It's a goodbye."

    henry "Whose?"

    ruth "Lydia Shaw is leaving Los Angeles tomorrow morning."

    henry "For where?"

    ruth "She says Portland."

    henry "You don't believe her."

    ruth "I believe she's leaving. We disagree about what that means."

    henry "What does she do?"

    ruth dry "She alters things."

    henry "Clothes?"

    ruth "Mostly people. Clothes when they're watching."

    "Henry drinks the office coffee. It has declined during the conversation."

    jump who_is_edwin


label who_is_edwin:
    henry "Who invited me?"

    ruth "I did."

    henry "Who invited you to invite me?"

    ruth "Edwin said I could bring one person."

    henry "Who is Edwin?"

    ruth composed "He runs the Winter Room. He believes seven is an accusation and eight is a dinner party."

    henry "Did you tell him what I do?"

    ruth "I told him you listen."

    henry "That's not what I do."

    ruth dry "It's why you get paid."

    henry "This is a job."

    ruth "No."

    henry "You came to my office."

    ruth "You never gave me your home address."

    henry "You never asked."

    ruth exposed "I am asking you to dinner, Henry. Don't make both requests humiliating."

    "It is the first time she has used his given name. He lets the fact pass without pretending not to notice it."

    jump one_person_there


label one_person_there:
    henry "Why me?"

    ruth composed "I need one person there who will not perform for them."

    henry "People perform for me."

    ruth "Not twice."

    henry "Who is “them”?"

    ruth "People who love Lydia in incompatible ways."

    henry "And you?"

    ruth dry "I'm compatible with everyone until they ask."

    henry "What do you think will happen?"

    ruth "A woman will leave Los Angeles."

    henry "That happens every day."

    ruth "Not when six people have built their lives around her staying."

    "Henry sets his coffee down beside hers. The two cups look like evidence from a meeting neither intends to admit occurred."

    jump what_she_is_afraid_of


label what_she_is_afraid_of:
    henry "What are you afraid of?"

    ruth exposed "She intends to forgive me."

    henry "For what?"

    ruth "She hasn't told me."

    henry "Have you apologized?"

    ruth shut "That's what makes it unpleasant."

    henry "Decline."

    ruth "If I don't go, she'll forgive me in writing."

    "A traffic signal changes outside. Red light passes across the frosted glass, then is gone."

    jump the_old_case


label the_old_case:
    henry "Last time we were in a room together, you told the truth and hated me for hearing it."

    ruth composed "I hated everyone else for making it necessary."

    henry "I was included."

    ruth dry "You usually are. You just stand far enough away to make it look accidental."

    henry "You said you would rather I heard it from you than found it."

    ruth shut "I remember what I said."

    henry "Does Frances still send you a card?"

    ruth "At Christmas. To the old address."

    "Henry takes his coat from the hook."

    jump change_the_shirt


label change_the_shirt:
    henry "Is there food at this dinner?"

    ruth dry "Edwin would consider the question actionable."

    henry "I'll change my shirt."

    ruth composed "That is the closest thing to friendship you've ever said to me."

    henry "Don't make me reconsider it."

    "Ruth picks up both coffee cups and carries them to the sink."

    hide ruth
    stop office
    end to_tollands
